n* 35.Reuter P., Disorganised Crime: The Economics of the Visible Hand (MIT Press, Cambridge, 1983). [Google Scholar]\n* 36.Campana P., Varese F., Organized crime in the United Kingdom: Illegal governance of markets and communities. Br. J. Criminol. 58, 1381–1400 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 37.Altai Consulting, Leaving Libya. Rapid Assessment of Municipalities of Departures of Migrants in Libya (Embassy of the Netherlands to Libya, Tripoli, 2017). [Google Scholar]\n* 38.UNODC, “Smuggling of migrants in the Sahel” (United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime, Vienna, 2022). [Google Scholar]\n* 39.Asmann P., Shuldiner H., El crimen organizado se beneficia del auge migratorio en Latinoamérica. Insighcrime.org, 26 December 2024.\n* 40.Brachet J., “From one stage to the next: Transit and transport in (trans) Saharan migrations” in African Migrations Research: Innovative Methods and Methodologies, Berriane M., de Haas H., Eds. (AfricaWorld, Trenton, NJ, 2012), pp. 91–114. [Google Scholar]\n* 41.Sanchez E. G., Zhang S. X., Rumors, encounters, collaborations, and survival: The migrant smuggling-drug trafficking nexus in the U.S. Southwest. Ann. Am. Acad. Polit. Soc. Sci. 676, 135–151 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 42.Lechleitner P. J., “Statement before the U.S. House of Representatives House Committee on Homeland Security Subcommittee on Border Security” (US Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Washington, DC, 2021), June 10. [Google Scholar]\n* 43.Frank-Vitale A., Coyotes, caravans, and the Central American migrant smuggling continuum. Trends Organ. Crime 26, 64–79 (2023). [Google Scholar]\n* 44.Loroña Celaya A., Sanchez G., “Una visión fronteriza sobre la interacción entre el tráfico de migrantes y el tráfico de drogas: El caso del Valle de Altar” in Reimagining the Migration Protection System: Critical Reflections from the Border, Sanchez G., Ed. (Hope Border Institute and Temple University, 2024), pp. 20–27. [Google Scholar]\n* 45.İçduygu A., Decentring migrant smuggling: Reflections on the Eastern Mediterranean route to Europe. J. Ethn. Migr. Stud. 47, 3293–3309 (2021). [Google Scholar]\n* 46.Gambetta D., Codes of the Underworld: How Criminals Communicate (Princeton University Press, Princeton, 2009). [Google Scholar]\n* 47.Massey D. S., Durand J., Pren K. A., Why border enforcement backfired. Am. J. Sociol. 121, 1557–1600 (2016). [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n* 48.Akerlof G. A., The market for ‘lemons’: Quality uncertainty and the market mechanism. Q. J. Econ. 84, 488–500 (1970). [Google Scholar]\n* 49.Optimity Advisors, “A study on smuggling of migrants” (European Commission, Brussels, 2015). [Google Scholar]\n* 50.Bilger V., Hofmann M., Jandl M., Human smuggling as a transnational service industry: Evidence from Austria. Int. Migr. 44, 59–93 (2006). [Google Scholar]\n* 51.Spener D., Clandestine Crossings: Migrants and Coyotes on the Texas-Mexico Border (Cornell University Press, 2009). [Google Scholar]\n* 52.Roberts Z., Information exchange between smugglers and migrants: An analysis of online interactions in Facebook groups (Criminal Justice, Borders and Citizenship Research Paper No. 3051186, 2017).\n* 53.Campana P., Gelsthorpe L., Choosing a smuggler: Decision-making amongst migrants smuggled to Europe. Eur. J. Crim. Policy Res. 27, 5–21 (2021). [Google Scholar]\n* 54.Düvell F., The ‘great Migration’ of summer 2015: Analysing the assemblage of key drivers in Turkey. J. Ethn. Migr. Stud. 45, 2227–2240 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 55.Álvarez Velasco S., “Ecuador” in Migrant Smuggling Data and Research: A Global Review of the Emerging Evidence Base, Triandafyllidou A., McAuliffe M., Eds. (IOM, Geneva, 2018). vol. 2. [Google Scholar]\n* 56.Majidi N., Community dimensions of smuggling: The case of Afghanistan and Somalia. Ann. Am. Acad. Polit. Soc. Sci. 676, 97–113 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 57.Stone-Cadena V., Álvarez Velasco S., Historicizing mobility: Coyoterismo in the indigenous Ecuadorian migration industry. Ann. Am. Acad. Polit. Soc. Sci. 676, 194–211 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 58.Hernández Campos C., Torre Cantalapiedra E., How can Mexican migrants reduce the risk of being abandoned by smugglers while clandestinely crossing the US–Mexico border? Migr. Stud. 10, 746–765 (2022). [Google Scholar]\n* 59.Harding A., Luu K., Clahane P., Smuggler selling ‘fast track’ Channel crossing speaks to BBC undercover reporter. BBC News. 28 October 2024a.\n* 60.Schelling T., The Strategy of Conflict (Harvard University Press, Cambridge, 1960). [Google Scholar]\n* 61.Campana P., Varese F., Cooperation in criminal organizations: Kinship and violence as credible commitments. Rational. Soc. 25, 263–289 (2013). [Google Scholar]\n* 62.Slack J., Martínez E. D., What makes a good human smuggler? The difference between satisfaction with and recommendation of coyotes on the U.S.-Mexico border. Ann. Am. Acad. Polit. Soc. Sci. 676, 152–173 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 63.Roberts B., Hanson G., Cornwell D., Borger S., “An analysis of migrant smuggling costs along the Southwest border” (US Office for Immigration Statistics, Washington, DC, 2010). [Google Scholar]\n* 64.Fuentes A., Garcia D., “Coyotaje: The structure and functioning of the people-smuggling industry” in Four Generations of Norteños: New Research from the Cradle of Mexican Migration, Cornelius W. A., Fitzgerald D., Borger S., Eds. (Center for Comparative Immigration Studies, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, 2009), pp. 79–102. [Google Scholar]\n* 65.Taylor D., Channel smugglers drop prices and cram more people on to boats. The Guardian, 6 August 2022. https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/aug/06/channel-smugglers-drop-prices-and-cram-more-people-on-to-boats.\n* 66.Zhang S. X., Sanchez G. E., Achilli L., Crimes of solidarity in mobility: Alternative views on migrant smuggling. Ann. Am. Acad. Polit. Soc. Sci. 676, 6–15 (2018). [Google Scholar]\n* 67.USSC, United States Sentencing Commission, “Alien smuggling offences. Quick facts” (2025). https://www.ussc.gov/research/quick-facts/alien-smuggling.\n* 68.Migration Observatory, “People crossing the English Channel in small boats” (Migration Observatory, University of Oxford, 2024), Policy Brief, 28 Jun 2024. [Google Scholar]\n* 69.NCA, NCA leads major increase in takedowns of social media accounts linked to people smuggling (National Crime Agency, London, 2025). [Google Scholar]\n* 70.IOM, “International Migration Law No. 34–Glossary on migration” (Internation Organisation for Migration, Geneva, 2019). [Google Scholar]\n* 71.Harding A., Luu K., Clahane P., Smuggler reveals operation to help Vietnamese reach UK. BBC News, 28 October 2024b.\n\n## Associated Data\n\nThis section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article.\n\n### Data Availability Statement\n\nThere are no data underlying this work.","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":8251.932688000124,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}